SELECT c.company_code, c.founder, COUNT(DISTINCT e.lead_manager_code), COUNT(DISTINCT e.senior_manager_code), COUNT(DISTINCT e.manager_code),COUNT(DISTINCT e.employee_code)
FROM Company c JOIN Employee e ON c.company_code = e.company_code
GROUP BY c.company_code, c.founder ORDER BY c.company_code;

COUNT(): is an aggregate function returns a single value.
e.g 
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM EMPLOYEES e; 
count all the employees in the employees table and return a single value.

SELECT COUNT(department) FROM EMPLOYEES e;
count the rows where department is not null.
